.env
.env.tastytrade
__pycache__/
*.pyc
circle_data/
circle_videos/
youtube_data/
circle_*.html
journal/signal_log_*.jsonl
journal/*.log
_extract_run.log
spec0_snippets.txt

# media — never in git (GitHub 100MB cap; 21GB+ total)
circle_audio/
research/audio_extracted/
discord_data/images/
*.wav
*.mp4

# ---- rescue pass 2026-08-19 ----
# CREDENTIALS. Never commit. Live OAuth token + Google client secret.
youtube_oauth_token.json
client_secret.json
*.credentials.json

# generated data - regenerated by scripts, never source
data_archive/
# 908 MB of 1-min price cache.
# `data/*`, NOT `data/` -- git never descends into an excluded DIRECTORY, so a
# `!data/...` re-include further down is dead with the trailing-slash form. That
# is how data/tradezella_trades.csv was lost. See the re-include near the bottom.
data/*
.cache/
discord_data/
_omen_mark_days.json

# derived research outputs. Files already tracked stay tracked; anything
# genuinely new and worth keeping goes in with: git add -f <path>
research/*.jsonl
research/*.bak
research/*.backup
research/*.raw
research/*.html
research/worklists.bak-*/
research/research/
research/.corpus_status_mark

# logs and scratch
*.log
*.err
*.bak
full_backtest_output.txt
_alp*.py
_probe*.py
_gate.py
_curate_commit.py
_extract_days.py
_patch_verdict.py
research/test.txt

# per-arm 2-year replay books (P7/G1). ~25 MB each and fully regenerable with
# `python research/p7_84_rule.py run --arm ...`; the same reason
# research/bt2y_trades.json has never been tracked. The finding lives in
# research/p7_84_rule.md, which IS tracked.
research/p7_arm_*.json

# per-arm 2-year replay books + the entry-bar probe (P8/G2), same story: tens of
# MB, regenerable with `python research/p8_scratch.py run --arm ...`, and
# research/p8_scratch.json is the flag-off verification replay from
# `python backtest_2y.py`. The finding lives in research/p8_scratch.md, which IS
# tracked -- the .md is deliberately outside this pattern.
research/p8_*.json

# generated decks: rebuild with research/build_deck.py, never commit the HTML
research/decks/**/*.html

# --- NEVER IGNORE A HUMAN JUDGEMENT -------------------------------------
# research/*.jsonl above exists for the tens of thousands of regenerable corpus
# artifacts. It is wider than it looks and has already silently swallowed real
# work twice: 5.2's T6 decks, and t60_silent_days.jsonl + rule_ballot_batch01.
# Austin's marks cannot be regenerated -- see CLAUDE.md, "never lose a mark".
!research/*marks*.jsonl
!research/mark_batch_*.jsonl
!research/*verdicts*.json
!research/*reviews*.jsonl
!research/rule_ballot_*.jsonl
!research/probe_*.jsonl
!research/t60_silent_days.jsonl
!research/marks/**
!research/probes/*.html
!research/decks/*-manifest.jsonl

# W4's candidate list: symbol-days nominated for grading, mined out of Austin's
# own Discord messages, the Circle "A+ Setups" space and the YouTube scrape.
# It holds NO grade and is NOT a mark corpus -- so it is deliberately absent from
# build_deck.LEGACY_MARK_FILES, which would otherwise mark these days as already
# judged and block the very cards W4 exists to produce. Tracked because
# research/w4_recall_sources.md cites it row by row.
!research/w4_candidate_days.jsonl

# Austin's TradeZella export: 350 hand-logged trades with entry, size, risk and
# realised R:R. NOT regenerable -- `data/` above is the 908 MB price cache and it
# swallowed this file once already (added at ce2a98d6, then silently lost; W6 had
# to recover it with `git show`). It is his hand, so it is a judgement file and
# THE ONE RULE applies. Scored by research/w6_tz_recall.py.
!data/tradezella_trades.csv

# G4 branch-attribution replay cache. ~22 MB and fully regenerable with
# `python research/g4_dropped_s.py`; the finding lives in research/g4_dropped_s.md,
# which IS tracked.
research/_g4_branches.json

# per-arm 2-year replay books for the ON WATCH A/B (T3/G3). ~40 MB each and
# regenerable with `python research/g3_onwatch_2y.py run --arm off|on`, same
# story as research/p7_arm_*.json above. The finding lives in
# research/g3_onwatch_2y.md, which IS tracked.
research/g3_arm_*.json

# G10 arm-event replay cache, regenerable with
# `python research/g10_arming_funnel.py run`; the finding lives in
# research/g10_arming_funnel.md, which IS tracked.
research/_g10_arm_events.json

# W12 coverage-sweep byproducts. `w12_trades.json` is a 39.5 MB replay book
# written only so the instrumented run has somewhere to put its output, and
# `.coverage` is coverage.py's binary database. Both regenerable with
# `python research/w12_reach.py run`; the finding lives in
# research/w12_bug_sweep.md and the READABLE result in research/_w12/cov.json,
# which IS tracked.
research/_w12/w12_trades.json
research/_w12/.coverage
research/_w12/*.log

# W3 per-arm 2-year replay books and their intermediates. ~40 MB each and
# regenerable with `python research/w3_recall_gate_fix_ab.py book --arm head|off|on`;
# the finding lives in research/w3_recall_gate_fix.md, which IS tracked.
research/w3_arm_*.json
research/_w3_*.json
research/_w3_*.log

# The morning artifact's summary block. research/*.html above exists for the
# generated pages (decks, probes, the tape itself), all of which rebuild from a
# script plus a book. This one is the opposite: hand-written source that a
# script CONSUMES -- `research/build_bt2y_report.py --summary` injects it above
# the scoreboard. Nothing regenerates it, so the wildcard would swallow it the
# way research/*.jsonl swallowed the 5.2 decks.
!research/h2_summary.html

# T11's re-scored 2-year book (research/t11_stop_fill_fix.md). 39.5 MB, and
# regenerable in one command -- same rule as research/bt2y_trades.json above:
#   ON_WATCH=1 python backtest_2y.py --days 730 --out research/t11_arm_ow1_closefill.json
# The numbers read off it live in research/_t11_rescore.json, which IS tracked.
research/t11_arm_ow1_closefill.json
.tasty_challenge

# T3's two 2-year books (research/t3_rule84-from-source.md), ~67 MB each and
# regenerable with the two commands the report gives verbatim -- same rule as
# research/bt2y_trades.json above. The numbers read off them live in the
# report itself and in research/t3_backtest_compare.py's output, both tracked.
research/t3_scratch/
